<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acronauplia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acronauplia is the rocky peninsula and oldest fortified quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nafplio</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Walled since antiquity, it was strengthened in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, the Venetians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and was the citadel of the town until the larger Palamidi fortress was built above it in the early 18th century. Remains of its successive walls survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acronauplia.jpg" alt="Acronauplia" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petroskaz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_view_from_Palamidi_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
